PATCH 547: DGROUP:0x6da4..0x6db0 is a run of thirteen BYTE variables -- the mission clock among them.

`FUN_1000_a5dc` is the mission supervisor.  Its first branch ends the mission when the countdown
word DGROUP:0x6da2 reaches zero, and its second ends it when the mission CLOCK reaches 00:00:00:

    1a5ef: 80 3e a6 6d ff    cmpb  $0xff,0x6da6      ; clock disabled?
    1a5f4: 74 22             je    0x1a618
    1a5f6: 80 3e a6 6d 00    cmpb  $0x0,0x6da6       ; minutes
    1a5fb: 75 1b             jne   0x1a618
    1a5fd: 80 3e a7 6d 00    cmpb  $0x0,0x6da7       ; seconds
    1a602: 75 14             jne   0x1a618
    1a604: 80 3e a8 6d 00    cmpb  $0x0,0x6da8       ; sub-seconds
    1a609: 75 0d             jne   0x1a618
    1a60b: c7 06 a0 6d 02 00 movw  $0x2,0x6da0       ; outcome 2 = TIME EXPIRED
    1a611: c7 06 a2 6d 02 00 movw  $0x2,0x6da2       ; ... arm the 2-tick teardown

The clock is armed at 0x47c7 (`movb $0x3c,0x6da6` / `movb $0x0,0x6da7` / `movb $0x0,0x6da8`), or per
mission from the limit table at 0x7b14 (`5167: mov 0x7b14(%bx),%al ; 516e: mov %al,0x6da6`), and
stepped by FUN_0000_4712 with a two-stage borrow (the decb/cmpb $0xff/movb $0x3b chain quoted at the
macro block).  Every access to all three is byte-width.

Ghidra typed the whole run 0x6da4..0x6db0 `undefined2`.  Because the thirteen are ADJACENT BYTES, each
macro aliases its successor:

    DAT_2000_2da6  covered bytes 0x6da6..0x6da7   (minutes + seconds)
    DAT_2000_2da7  covered bytes 0x6da7..0x6da8   (seconds + sub-seconds)
    DAT_2000_2da8  covered bytes 0x6da8..0x6da9   (sub-seconds + a live flag)

so `DAT_2000_2da8 = DAT_2000_2da8 - 1` stepped a 16-bit value 0x0000 -> 0xffff and the underflow test
never matched.  The borrow never propagated, the clock never reached zero, and outcome 2 was
unreachable.  Measured over 3.19M cooperative ticks of AZER1 before this patch:

    [end] t=3186000 out=0000 tmr=ffff | 6da6=0f 6da7=00 6da8=01 | ... | e814=00
    [end] t=3188000 out=0000 tmr=ffff | 6da6=0f 6da7=00 6da8=f4 | ... | e814=00

-- 0x6da8 walks straight through 0x00 and wraps to 0xf4 instead of reloading to 0x3b, and 0x6da7 is
still 0x00 after 45 minutes of mission clock.  No mission could ever time out.

The evidence for the run being bytes is exhaustive rather than local.  Classifying every memory
operand in the image (branch targets excluded) gives, for the offsets bracketing and covering it:

    0x6da0  word 11  byte  0      <- genuine word (outcome code)
    0x6da2  word 15  byte  0      <- genuine word (teardown countdown)
    0x6da4  word  0  byte  2
    0x6da5  word  0  byte  3
    0x6da6  word  0  byte  9
    0x6da7  word  0  byte  8
    0x6da8  word  0  byte  6
    0x6da9  word  0  byte  3
    0x6daa  word  0  byte  5
    0x6dab  word  0  byte 50
    0x6dac  word  0  byte  7
    0x6dad  word  0  byte  7
    0x6dae  word  0  byte 17
    0x6daf  word  0  byte  3
    0x6db0  word  0  byte  4
    0x6db2  word  7  byte  0      <- genuine word
    0x6db4  word 18  byte  0      <- genuine word

122 byte accesses, zero word accesses, cleanly bracketed by real words on both sides.

The aliasing damaged more than the clock: 0x6da9 and 0x6daa are live byte flags (`movb $0x1,0x6da9`
at 0xe8fc, `cmpb $0x1,0x6da9` at 0x1633c, `movb $0x1,0x6daa` at 0x1630a, `cmpb $0x1,0x6daa` at
0xe864/0x1bb5c/0x1bbe6), so `DAT_2000_2da9 = 1` also zeroed 0x6daa, and `DAT_2000_2daa = 1` also
zeroed 0x6dab -- the editor/pause flag that a5dc itself branches on at 0x1a678 and that fifty other
sites test.  Narrowing the run removes all of it at once.

The three `== -1` in FUN_0000_4712 and the `!= 0xffff` in FUN_1000_a5dc are repaired in the same
patch because the narrowing forces them: the asm constant is the byte 0xff, and an unsigned char
promotes to int, so `== -1` is a constant false.

